  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx={2,\\,4,\\,6,\\,8},\\,y={1,\\,3,\\,5,\\,7}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{\u2015}{y}=4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$",
  "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x={{x}_{1},{x}_{2},\u2026,{x}_{n}}$ and $y={{y}_{1},{y}_{2},\u2026,{y}_{n}}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{\u2015}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{\u2015}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-5)(1-4)+(4-5)(3-4)+(6-5)(5-4)+(8-5)(7-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(-3)+(-1)(-1)+(1)(1)+(3)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[9+1+1+9] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
